          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which mechanism of horizontal gene transfer is described as clinically the most </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which mechanism of horizontal gene transfer is described as clinically the most important route for the rapid spread of resistance genes such as NDM-1 between unrelated bacterial species in intensive care units?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Conjugation via plasmid transfer</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Conjugation transfers plasmids directly between bacteria through a sex pilus, allowing resistance genes to spread rapidly across even unrelated species — this is why NDM-1 moved so quickly through ICUs.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Transduction by bacteriophage</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Not the main route here. Transduction relies on a bacteriophage carrying the gene between cells, which is far slower and less common in ICU outbreaks than plasmid conjugation.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Transformation from free environmental DNA</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Transformation requires bacteria to pick up naked DNA from their surroundings — it occurs, but it is not the dominant route for rapid multi-species spread like NDM-1.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Spontaneous chromosomal mutation</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Mutation only passes resistance vertically to daughter cells of the same organism — it cannot explain a gene appearing across unrelated species, which requires horizontal transfer.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Review the three horizontal transfer routes: conjugation (plasmid via pilus) is the one responsible for rapid cross-species spread of genes like NDM-1."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct — conjugation is the clinically dominant horizontal transfer route driving multidrug resistance outbreaks."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
