        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 270.8,
            "to": 280.8
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A clinician needs to confirm whether a standard antibiotic dose will actually re</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A clinician needs to confirm whether a standard antibiotic dose will actually reach an effective concentration at the infection site. Which AST method should be requested to get this exact value?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Kirby-Bauer disk diffusion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Disk diffusion is cheap and widely used, but it only classifies the isolate as Susceptible, Intermediate, or Resistant — it does not give an exact concentration value.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Broth microdilution</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Broth microdilution is the gold standard because it determines the Minimum Inhibitory Concentration (MIC) — the lowest drug concentration that stops visible growth — letting the clinician check if a standard dose achieves that level at the infection site.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Automated VITEK 2 system</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>VITEK 2 gives rapid results within hours and is very useful clinically, but broth microdilution remains the reference (gold standard) method for MIC determination.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Molecular resistance gene detection</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Molecular tests detect the presence of a resistance gene directly, which is fast for high-priority organisms, but they do not quantify the Minimum Inhibitory Concentration.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember: disk diffusion only gives a category (S/I/R); broth microdilution is the gold standard for the exact MIC."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct — broth microdilution is the gold standard because it yields the exact MIC value."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
