          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 25-year-old woman has an uncomplicated lower urinary tract infection caused by</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 25-year-old woman has an uncomplicated lower urinary tract infection caused by ESBL-producing E. coli, and the isolate is reported as susceptible. Per current IDSA/ICMR guidance, what is the most appropriate first-line therapy?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>IV meropenem</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This is the reflexive carbapenem over-use the video warns against — using a last-resort drug for an uncomplicated infection drives further resistance and should be reserved for complicated or systemic disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Oral fosfomycin</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. For uncomplicated ESBL E. coli cystitis, oral fosfomycin (or nitrofurantoin) is preferred when the isolate is susceptible, preserving carbapenems for complicated or high-MIC infections.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>IV ceftriaxone</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ESBL enzymes destroy extended-spectrum cephalosporins like ceftriaxone, so this would fail — ESBL isolates resist penicillins and cephalosporins by definition.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>IV colistin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Colistin is a last-resort agent reserved for extensively or pan-drug-resistant organisms, not for a straightforward susceptible ESBL cystitis case.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Recall the distinction: pyelonephritis or systemic infection needs IV carbapenem, but simple ESBL cystitis should be treated with oral fosfomycin or nitrofurantoin to avoid carbapenem over-use."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct — oral fosfomycin/nitrofurantoin is first-line for uncomplicated ESBL cystitis; carbapenems are reserved for complicated or systemic infection."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
